## 2. Specifics of the Infrastructure
What is a server?
A server is a physical or virtual computer system running an operating system (such as Linux) that provides computational resources, services, programs, or data to other computers (clients) over a network.

What is the role of the domain name?
A domain name (e.g., foobar.com) provides a human-readable address to identify a website on the Internet instead of requiring users to memorize raw numerical IP addresses (e.g., 8.8.8.8).

What type of DNS record www is in www.foobar.com?
Since www.foobar.com directly maps to the IPv4 address 8.8.8.8, it is configured as an A record (Address Record). (Alternatively, if www.foobar.com points to foobar.com, it would be a CNAME record).

What is the role of the web server?
The Web Server (Nginx) manages inbound HTTP/HTTPS connections from client browsers. It accepts requests, handles SSL/TLS termination, serves static assets (HTML, CSS, JS, images), and acts as a reverse proxy by routing dynamic requests to the Application Server.

What is the role of the application server?
The Application Server provides the execution runtime environment for the website's dynamic code. It processes business logic, executes scripts (PHP, Python, Node.js, etc.), queries the database, and dynamically generates HTTP responses to pass back to Nginx.

What is the role of the database?
The Database (MySQL) is a Relational Database Management System (RDBMS) responsible for persistently storing, querying, updating, and managing site data (such as user accounts, posts, and dynamic content).

What is the server using to communicate with the computer of the user requesting the website?
The communication relies on the TCP/IP protocol suite, specifically using HTTP (Port 80) or HTTPS (Port 443) over TCP sockets.

## 3. Issues with this Infrastructure
Single Point of Failure (SPOF):
Because all infrastructure components (Nginx, App Server, MySQL) run on a single physical/virtual machine, any hardware failure, OS crash, or network outage instantly brings down the entire website.

Downtime during Maintenance:
Performing software updates, code deployments, or restarting services (such as Nginx or MySQL) requires interrupting server operations, causing immediate downtime for users.

Inability to Scale (Scalability Bottleneck):
A single machine can only scale vertically (by adding CPU, RAM, or storage). Under heavy traffic spikes, CPU/RAM resources will saturate, leading to slow response times or complete request drops because the architecture  cannot scale horizontally across multiple servers.